cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this formalization, please cite it as below."
title: "AlphaCurvature: Lean 4 proofs of the Gibilisco–Isola conjectures on the scalar curvature of the classical and quantum α-geometries"
authors:
  - family-names: Bornemann
    given-names: Adam
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
  - name: "Claude (Anthropic)"
version: 2.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-23
license: Apache-2.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.24464060
repository-code: "https://github.com/Charmooo/AlphaCurvature"
type: software
abstract: >-
  Formal proofs, in Lean 4 with Mathlib, of Conjectures 4.1 and 4.2 of
  P. Gibilisco and T. Isola (J. Math. Phys. 46 (2005)). With p = 2/(1 − α), the
  α-geometry is the pullback of the Euclidean (Hilbert–Schmidt) metric by the
  Amari embedding ρ ↦ p ρ^(1/p), and by log ρ at p = ∞. Conjecture 4.1: on the
  open probability simplex with at least three points, its scalar curvature is
  strictly Schur-increasing for p in (2, ∞] and strictly Schur-decreasing for p
  in (1, 2). Conjecture 4.2: on the faithful states of n × n matrices, its scalar
  curvature is a strictly Schur-monotone function of the eigenvalues in the same
  directions, for every n. The classical proof formalizes a closed form of the
  scalar curvature in power sums, applies the Schur–Ostrowski criterion, and
  splits the gradient numerator into one explicit term per point. The quantum
  proof computes the curvature of the image of the embedding by the Gauss
  equation, splits it into the classical curvature of the eigenvalues plus a
  quantum excess, writes the off-diagonal kernel as a power-mean integral, and
  shows that the excess is strictly Schur-monotone. The endpoint p = ∞ of
  Conjecture 4.1 and the slice p = 3 of Conjecture 4.2 were formalized earlier in
  the trureturing project (github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing, pull
  requests 15566 and 15831).
keywords:
  - Lean 4
  - Mathlib
  - formal verification
  - information geometry
  - quantum information geometry
  - α-geometry
  - Amari embedding
  - scalar curvature
  - majorization
  - Schur-convexity
